---
/**
 * Contact, EN. Twin of `src/pages/contact.astro`.
 *
 * ⚠ NOT A CONVERSION PAGE — see the French twin for the full reasoning. Short
 * version: `/en/privacy/` promises a right of access, correction, withdrawal of
 * consent and deletion, and points here to exercise it. Quebec's private-sector
 * privacy law applies to whoever collects the information, individuals
 * included, and requires the person responsible to be IDENTIFIABLE. Without a
 * real name and a reachable channel on this page, that promise is hollow
 * (`docs/HANDOFF-TENANT.md` §4.1).
 *
 * So: no form here, no button call to action, no promise of a callback.
 *
 * The name and address come from `PRIVACY_OFFICER` (src/lib/constants.ts) so
 * the two twins cannot drift and the tenant handoff changes one place.
 *
 * Rule 1: no phone, no address. There is no premises.
 *
 * The title is "Contact us", not "Contact": the word is identical in both
 * languages, and `seo-audit.mjs` fails hard on a duplicate <title> anywhere in
 * the build — correctly, since two pages competing on one title is precisely
 * what that check exists to catch.
 */
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { PRIVACY_OFFICER } from '@lib/constants.ts';
import { pathFor } from '@lib/routes.ts';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '@lib/schema.ts';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const ref = { type: 'static', page: 'contact' } as const;
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  ref={ref}
  title="Contact us — Solage Capitale"
  description="Who to write to, and what for: the person responsible for personal information, exercising your rights, and reporting a factual error on a page."
  schema={[
    breadcrumbSchema(locale, [
      { name: "Home", ref: { type: 'home' } },
      { name: "Contact", ref },
    ]),
  ]}
>
  <section class="contenant bloc-page">
    <div class="mesure">
      <h1>Contact</h1>

      <p class="chapeau">
        One email address, with a named person behind it. This page reaches the site's editor —
        about your personal information, or to report an error. To have a foundation looked at,
        use the <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'quote' }, locale)}>quote request</a>
        instead.
      </p>

      <h2>Writing to the site's editor</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>{PRIVACY_OFFICER.name}</strong><br />
        <a href={`mailto:${PRIVACY_OFFICER.email}`}>{PRIVACY_OFFICER.email}</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Solage Capitale is published by an individual, not by a construction company, and that
        individual is the person <strong>responsible for the personal information</strong> the
        quote form collects. Quebec law requires that, and above all it requires that person to
        be identifiable — hence a name here rather than an anonymous inbox.
      </p>

      <h2>Exercising your rights over your information</h2>
      <p>
        If you have already sent a quote request through this site, you may ask to
        <strong>access</strong> the information held about you, to have it
        <strong>corrected</strong>, to <strong>withdraw your consent</strong>, or to have it
        <strong>deleted</strong>. Just say so, at the address above. What is collected and why
        is set out in the
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'privacy' }, locale)}>privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a request to go anywhere, the original submission has to be findable: give the email
        address you used in the form and, if you have it, the approximate date. Without those
        two, there is nothing to look up — the site creates no account and keeps no identifier
        that would let it recognise you any other way.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the answer does not satisfy you, the Commission d'accès à l'information du Québec is
        the authority competent to receive a complaint.
      </p>

      <h2>Reporting an error on a page</h2>
      <p>
        The site cites the Régie du bâtiment du Québec, the Bureau de normalisation du Québec
        and the new home warranty plan, and every claim it makes is tied to its public source. A
        source can be amended, a standard can be revised, and a reading can simply be wrong.
        <strong>If you find an error, reporting it is useful</strong> — name the page and the
        passage, and the correction is made at the source rather than only on the page where it
        happened to be spotted.
      </p>

      <h2>What this channel is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It is not an emergency line.</strong> Email is not monitored continuously. If
          there is water in the basement right now, the
          <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'emergency' }, locale)}>emergency page</a>
          covers what to do first and who to call.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It is not a diagnosis service.</strong> Nobody can rule on a foundation from a
          description or a photograph, and this site provides no technical advice and no
          engineering opinion. A crack is assessed on site.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It is not the contractor.</strong> The work is carried out by an independent
          company holding its own RBQ licence. Who does what is set out in the
          <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'terms' }, locale)}>terms of use</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Requesting a quote</h2>
      <p>
        To have a drain, a crack or an infiltration assessed, the
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'quote' }, locale)}>quote request form</a>
        collects what a contractor needs in order to call you back usefully. It is free and
        carries no obligation, and you remain free to seek other quotes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
